eads like a time travel book  but there's not actually time travel  taking place rather it's travel sort of  between contemporaneous universes or  instances of the universe did you start  with that as the concept and then work  out the story and characters or did you  start maybe with a character or a plot  idea I started with the idea of the box  itself once I had a grasp on quantum  mechanics I was wondering how would I  write something that allowed a character  to move between worlds and the notion of  the box came to me as I was actually  brainstorming something else and it was  such a strong visual image idea of this  box in a hangar with lights shining down  on it and I mean one of the one of the  interesting things about quantum  mechanics is to make particles exist in  multiple places at the same time we have  to put them in isolation we can't look  at them you have to turn the temperature  down to the absolute zero there can be  no outside interference and once you do  that they're moving across different  realities and and I just wanted to scale  that up so I had idea for this box and  and once I realized that that was my way  into the book the rest of it came pretty  easily do you start knowing the full  story arc and how it's going to go and I  think that with dark matter it's  particularly relevant because of again  without giving anything away about  specific plot points because of the  development of the story it seems to me  as someone who cannot write fiction to  save my life that it would be very  difficult to start writing this without  having a sense of where it's going to go  and ultimately end up well I always have  a sense of where the books are going  when I start I at least know what's  happening up through the midpoints and I  might have an idea or two about the last  act the third act and what happens then  but that almost always changes for dark  matter when I was selling it and I was  explaining to publishers and movie  studios what I thought was going to be  happening in the third act that ended up  changing dramatically  what it is in the book and I'm not gonna  spoil it because I think it's a lot of  fun when the reader encounters that plot  twist but you know it's funny I was  looking back the way I came to that plot  twist as I was looking back through all  of my writing journals and I I spent  several months just taking notes on a  book before I start it and typically  when I get ready to try to finish the  book I'll go back and I'll just look  through my notes and just to see if I  missed anything  were there any kernels of genius in  there that I just forgot to put in the  book and I was really struggling with  third act of dark matter and then I went  back and like on the third day I had  been brainstorming it I had just dashed  off this little note about what happens  to Jason when he gets back to his world  that's what I'm gonna say and it was  exactly the end but I remember writing  it at that point in time thinking oh  that's just too crazy that would never  work and it ended up being exactly what  the book needed but I had to write  though most of the book to realize that  so plot lines aside there's some pretty  pretty dark stuff in in dark matter that  that is experienced by the protagonist  you know I was thinking as I read it  about how we often hear this idea that  comedians are often actually depressed  dark people who are in difficult places  in their life and have suffered through  a lot  I mean we're how does your personal  background connect to some of the  darkness and and difficult stuff that  exists in dark matter or does it yeah  and horror and thriller writers are  actually some of the happiest people  even if they write the darkest stuff and  is it's a inverse thing from comedians I  think um no but in a lot of my books  it's sort of like I think of it as  therapy in Reverse and I don't really  understand what I'm doing at the time  when I'm writing the book in terms of  what am i working out in terms of my own  issues but by the time I get to the end  of the book I have a pretty clear sense  of oh well this is PI I was writing  about that because I'm clearly going  through this thing in my life and it's  not something I try to look too closely  at during the writing of the book  because I think it would be it would  overshadow the  and the characters and all of that stuff  I was trying to pull off but it's  something I've been more and more aware  of over the last several books I want to  talk about some of your other projects  but real quick  are you experiencing that there's some  people confused between your novel and  the TV series by the same name based on  a comic book that's sort of a space  opera the other day I mentioned to  someone I'd be interviewing you they  said oh I recently read the book and I  haven't yet seen the TV series on sci-fi  which of course is another thing  altogether is that is there any  interesting backstory to any of that or  confusion you've seen people have no I  mean I just heard names might think it's  my book spurred the TV show but no I  think if I recall that TV show is in  development when I sold the book I mean  now almost three years ago yeah so you  know they were doing their thing and you  know I was working on this book I mean I  hopefully when the win and if the movie  comes out that won't be any more  confusion okay so let's talk about the  wayward Pines trilogy that was adapted  into a TV series for Fox M night  Shyamalan was the executive producer  when you I'm sorry he also directed the  pilot episode also directed the pilot  episode correct  so when M night Shyamalan you find out  is going to be involved in a project of  turning something you wrote into  something for television is that hugely  exciting or do you also share do you  also have some concerns that it will  become more something in the style of M  night Shyamalan than what you originally  sort of conceived it to be or somewhere  in between I think well I wasn't really  that concerned because I think nights in  my sensibilities are actually very  similar and where I've gotten to know  him that's become apparent our  storytelling instincts happen to line up  in a lot of instances and I think that's  one of the reasons he was attracted to  where it Pines is because it has this  huge twist that no one sees coming and  it was funny when we had first sent the  script with  episode 2 him offering it to him to  consider coming on board to direct the  pilot episode and be an executive  producer he read the script and as you  know the first seat the first episode  ends with Ethan Burke trying to drive  out of Witter Pines not being able to  and it's just this big cliffhanger  moment and night had said as long as  they're not all dead I'm in so I think  he's got this sense of kind of the big  sweeping plot movements hello I still  remember going to see the sixth sense in  when I was in college and walking out of  that theater just blown away by it that  audacious storytelling so I was thrilled  when he came on board I'm even more  excited to when the show turned out to  be as good as it was we were able to  find that audience do there sometimes  have to be sort of candid conversations  where it's sort of communicated to you  listen we understand this is how this  plot element or scene or story component  is in the books but here's why it just  can't be this way when it's on  television are there some components  that are like that yeah often it's it's  me telling the writers and the producers  that because you know there's a instinct  sometimes where there where and I know  it often stories are told that's the  opposite of what I'm going to say but  where people are trying are trying to be  too faithful to the material and not  being willing to take a big departure  where it needs to and I think that's one  of the reasons I've been able to work  well with Hollywood is because I I'm not  Precious about my book once the book is  finished the book is its own thing and  while clearly and every project I write  like there are scenes that absolutely  should be a movie or should be in the TV  show but then there are other scenes  that it really only works in prose and  it wouldn't work visually and I'm aware  of that  in in the production process and often  trying to steer people away from being  too faithful to the source material like  before we let you go are you watching  any TV  shows now what do you watch for fun what  am i watching right now  I just watched flea bag on Amazon a  really funny scathing British comedy but  of course I've been devouring the new  season twin piece  mm-hmm many peaks was a big inspiration  for me growing up I mean it basically I  grew wayward Pines because of Twin Peaks  I thought I would never be a third  season would never happen I just wanted  to write something to make me feel the  way to my Peaks that I think I think  that is one of the coolest most  beautiful things that's ever been made  film or TV so you know that's those are  the two things I'm kind of into at the  moment and I'm just trying to finish my  next book all right we've been speaking  with blake crouch author screenwriter  producer of wayward pines good behavior  he wrote dark matter when can we expect  the next book ah that's a good question  I am hoping sometime maybe late next  year it just depends I need to I'm not  finished with it yet you know might  these books are are taking longer and  longer there's a bit of science and the  new women is it is a very serpentine  complex mind-bending story structure so  I'm really taking my time with it  because science you know I want to I  want to do right by this idea awesome I  look forward to it Blake thanks for  taking time to talk to me today my  pleasure  okay

